# Hub pages - legacy hash anchors now have real pages, but preserve anchor jumps
# If someone visits old hash URL, client-side JS handles scroll; no redirect needed

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO TRAILING-SLASH REDIRECTS. Ever.
#
# These lines used to sit here, one per hub:
#
#   /composer/  /composer  301
#   /work/      /work      301
#   /reel/      /reel      301
#   /process/   /process   301
#   /services/  /services  301
#   /contact/   /contact   301
#
# Every route above is served from <route>/index.html, and Cloudflare Pages
# answers a slashless request for a directory route with its own 308 to the
# trailing-slash form. So the 301 pointed straight back into that 308:
#
#   /work -> 308 /work/ -> 301 /work -> 308 /work/ -> ... ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS
#
# The page never loaded. Every REEL / WORK / COMPOSER / PROCESS / RATES /
# contact link in the site - including the whole primary nav on /store - was
# dead for as long as those rules were live (verified 2026-09-19: /work
# answered 308, /reel/ answered 301, the pair is a loop).
#
# Both URL forms are served as 200s instead: <route>/index.html renders
# /route/ natively, and the generated root twin <route>.html renders the
# canonical slashless /route exactly the way store.html already renders
# /store. Regenerate the twins with `node tools/route-aliases.mjs` after
# editing a hub page (tools/check-sitemap.mjs fails if they drift).
# _headers gives both forms the same cache policy and a rel="canonical"
# Link header back to the slashless canonical, and sitemap.xml lists the
# slashless form, so crawlers consolidate on /route with no redirect hop.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ITEM ENTRY ROUTES - /work/<slug>, /reel/<slug>, /store/<slug> (65 pages)
#
# Every entry is a flat file: work/expire.html, reel/needle-in-the-nerve.html,
# store/anesthesia-for-the-signal-age.html, ... Cloudflare Pages serves the
# extensionless slashless form straight from the file with a 200 (the same
# mechanism /store.html has always used), and 308s the ".html" form to it, so
# there is nothing to redirect here and nothing to normalise. No trailing-slash
# rule may ever be added for them: a directory would bring back the 308 the
# slashless canonical has to avoid. The URLs are generated, never typed - see
# src/data and scripts/generate-sitemap.mjs.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THERE IS STILL NO `/* /404.html 404` RULE
#
# Cloudflare Pages _redirects supports redirects (3xx) only - a rule with a 404
# destination is a rewrite, which the platform does not implement
# (https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/ - "Rewrites
# (other status codes)": not supported). Worse, "Redirects are always followed,
# regardless of whether or not an asset matches the incoming request", so a
# `/*` splat would shadow every real page on the site.
#
# A real 404 needs no rule at all: Cloudflare Pages disables SPA mode as soon as
# a 404.html file exists in the output, and answers every unmatched path with
# 404.html and HTTP 404. `_headers` pins X-Robots-Tag: noindex, follow on
# /404.html so the error template can never be indexed as a page.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO RENAMES. The 19 URLs that existed before this build (/work, /reel,
# /store, ...) keep their exact paths, their canonicals and their sitemap
# entries; the 65 entry URLs are additions, never replacements.
